{
  "slug": "2phases",
  "seriesSlug": "standalone",
  "sequence": 0,
  "title": "The Two Phases of Salvation",
  "description": "The New Testament talks of two phases of salvation that are related but distinct: (1) what God did to mankind in Christ's holy history (You in Christ), and (2) what God is presently doing in the believer (Christ in You). By E.H. Jack Sequeira",
  "keywords": [
    "Jesus Christ",
    "salvation",
    "imputed righteousness",
    "imparted righteousness",
    "fruits of the gospel"
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "1. Importance of the Study"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "The New Testament talks of two phases of salvation that are related but distinct. The following is a brief explanation of these two phases of salvation:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "What God did to mankind in Christ’s holy history – "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "You in Christ.",
            "marks": [
              "strong"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": " "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": " "
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "What God is presently doing in the believer – "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "Christ in You.",
            "marks": [
              "strong"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": " "
          }
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "These are not two gospels but two distinct aspects of salvation [Jn. 15:4,5; Rev. 3:20]. In both, Christ is our righteousness and both constitute an essential part of the Chrsitian message of salvation [Rom. 8:9,10]. The first deals with the "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "objective",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " facts of the gospel (imputed righteousness); the second with the "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "subjective",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " experience of salvation (imparted righteousness)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "These two phases of salvation must be kept distinct to avoid confusion between the "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "gospel",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " (that which saves or justifies) and the "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "fruits of the gospel",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " (that which produces holy living or sanctifies). "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "You in Christ",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " is the gospel message and refers to what God has "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "already",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " done for us in Christ, i.e., salvation full and complete. "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Christ in you,",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " on the other hand, has to do with the Christian life and is an "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "ongoing",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " process, i.e., what Christ is continually doing in us through His Spirit [Gal. 5:22, 23]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Truth and morality, knowledge and action are distinct but inseparably connected, like the root of a tree and its fruit. Salvation must express itself in behavior, and behavior must embody salvation. Genuine justification by faith "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "always",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " produces works [Ja. 2:14-26]. It is for this reason the New Testament teaches that we are justified by faith "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "alone",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " [Rom. 3:28; Gal. 2:16], but judged by works [Matt. 7:21; 25:34-40; Jn. 5:28-30; Rom. 2:5-8; 2 Cor. 5:10], the works being the evidence of justification by faith [Eph. 2:8-10; Ja. 2:20-22]. Only when these two phases of salvation are clearly understood, kept distinct, and yet linked together, does the gospel become "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "the power of God unto salvation.",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "2. Distinction Between ‘You in Christ’ and ‘Christ in You’"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "The following are the main distinctions between the objective facts of the gospel, "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "You in Christ,",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " and its fruits, its subjective experience, "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Christ in You:",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "‘YOU IN CHRIST’ – Objective",
          "marks": [
            "strong"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " (imputed righteousness) "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "‘CHRIST IN YOU’ – Subjective",
          "marks": [
            "strong"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " (imparted righteousness) "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " A."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "COMPLETE"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " We stand perfect in Christ"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "1 Cor. 6:11; Eph. 1:3-6; Col. 2:10"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " A."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "ONGOING"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " We are growing up in Christ"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Phil. 3:12-14; Col. 1:27; 2:6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " B."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "UNIVERSAL"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " Includes all mankind"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Rom. 5:18; 2 Cor. 5:19; Tit. 2:11"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " B."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "PARTICULAR"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " Applies only to believers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Jn. 3:16; Rom. 8:9,10; 1 Tim. 4:10"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " C."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "UNCONDITIONAL"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " By grace alone"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Rom. 3:21,28; 1 Cor.1:30,31; Phil. 3:9"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " C."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "CONDITIONAL"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " By faith alone"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Lk. 9:23; Gal. 2:20; Phil. 2:12,13"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " D."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "MERITORIOUS"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " That which justifies and qualifies us for heaven"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Acts 15:5-11; Eph. 2:8, 9; Tit. 3:5"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " D."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "DEMONSTRATIVE"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " That which sanctifies and witnesses our justification"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Jn. 14:12; Eph. 2:10; Tit. 3:8; Ja. 2:17, 20-26"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "children": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Note:",
          "marks": [
            "strong"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " The phrase "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "in Christ",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " (the central theme of Paul’s theology) is applied objectively as well as subjectively in the New Testament. But it must be remembered, that the subjective experience is "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "always",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " based on the objective facts of the gospel; that which was realized in the holy history of Christ [1 Cor. 3:11]. The fundamental truth of the "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "in Christ",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " motif is that God united our corporate humanity, that needed redeeming, to Christ’s divinity at the incarnation (thus qualifying Him to be the second Adam), and then re-wrote our history in His Son’s life, death, and resurrection. This gave mankind a new status in which we stand legally justified, i.e., reconciled to God and accepted "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "in the Beloved",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " [1 Cor. 1:30; Eph. 1:3-6]. By faith this truth of legal justification is made effective; hence, it is referred to as "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "justification by faith",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " (which is in complete opposition to "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "justification by works,",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": " see Rom. 9:30-33). This results in the new birth experience "
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "(Christ in you)",
          "marks": [
            "em"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "value": ", which in turn is manifested by holy living (i.e., sanctification); the reproduction of Christ’s righteous life in us, the fruits of justification by faith [Jn. 15:1-5]."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "sourcePath": "jacksequeira.org/2phases.htm",
  "language": "en"
}